{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 399,
    "dDurationMs": 2461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2860,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes are a paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4540,
    "dDurationMs": 6579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are paradoxical because they simultaneously must exist but can’t, and so they break"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11119,
    "dDurationMs": 2041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics as we know it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13160,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But many physicists will tell you that the best way to fix broken physics is with string."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18590,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "String theory, in fact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20390,
    "dDurationMs": 6177,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in the black holes of string theory - fuzzballs - are perhaps even weirder than the regular type"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31289,
    "dDurationMs": 4091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s general theory of relativity\ntells us that if the density of matter is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35380,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sufficiently ridiculous, ultimate gravitational\ncollapse is inevitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39890,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The matter should contract to a single point\nof infinite density - the singularity - which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44910,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is surrounded by a surface from which even\nlight can’t escape - the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50640,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we see these black holes - or at least\ntheir incontrovertible evidence - in many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55570,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "places out there in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57100,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But black holes are also impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60200,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the central singularity, the known laws\nof physics break down - general relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65540,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comes into irreconcilable conflict with quantum\nmechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68360,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s our first paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70850,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at the event horizon another paradox arises."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74180,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the one hand, general relativity insists\nthat no information from the matter that fell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78090,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a black hole should be observable on\nthe surface - the so-called no-hair theorem,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83880,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which says that the only properties that we\ncan observe from outside a black hole are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88350,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its mass, electric charge, and angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91500,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s not very many bits of information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94350,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, if we calculate the amount of information that goes into a black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98850,
    "dDurationMs": 8290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to build it up, we get a prodigiously large\nnumber - 10^10^77 bits for a black hole the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107140,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass of our Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108680,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes should have an enormous number of so-called microstates - hidden configurations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114340,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and this translates to an enormous entropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118340,
    "dDurationMs": 5069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The formula for black hole entropy was discovered by Jakob Bekenstein, who, incidentally, also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123409,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inspired the colourful phrasing that black\nholes “have no hairs” to describe the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128789,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absence of observable microstates in a black hole, which, on the surface, seems to contradict"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134590,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his black hole entropy formula."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136470,
    "dDurationMs": 1919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s an analogy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138389,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can describe the behavior of the air in\nthis room with very few numbers: its bulk"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143989,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion, its temperature, pressure, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147129,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these few numbers emerge from the motion of every air molecule — and to describe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152120,
    "dDurationMs": 5839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we’d need the positions and momenta\nof 10^27ish particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157959,
    "dDurationMs": 6581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the air has the very high entropy of lots\nof information that is hidden under very few"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164540,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "macroscopic properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165840,
    "dDurationMs": 6935,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the case of air, the hidden information still exists and is, in principle, still measurable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172775,
    "dDurationMs": 3774,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not so in the black holes of general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176549,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The no-hair theorem says that there’s no\ninformation beyond charge, mass and spin that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181840,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observable above the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184400,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The information contributing to black hole\nentropy could be beneath the horizon, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189180,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we add one more fact we run into serious\ntrouble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193290,
    "dDurationMs": 3839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes evaporate by emitting Hawking\nradiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197129,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about this previously, but the TL;DW is that Hawking radiation should be completely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202909,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "random, and so leaks away the black hole’s\nmass without any of the information that went"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207430,
    "dDurationMs": 1679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into building the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209109,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the black hole eventually vanishes, so\ndoes its enormous information content."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214199,
    "dDurationMs": 6441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that violates a very deep and fundamental law - the law of conservation of quantum information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220640,
    "dDurationMs": 5128,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This threat of erasure of quantum information in a hairless black hole is "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225768,
    "dDurationMs": 2391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole information paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228159,
    "dDurationMs": 3231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah, black holes seem to be paradoxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231390,
    "dDurationMs": 1679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet they definitely exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233069,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you know what doesn’t exist?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236079,
    "dDurationMs": 1401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actual paradoxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237480,
    "dDurationMs": 4799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we see an apparent paradox in physics, it’s really a clue pointing to a gap in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242279,
    "dDurationMs": 1850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our understanding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244129,
    "dDurationMs": 2881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solving the paradox can lead us to new knowledge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247010,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While we’ve covered these black hole paradoxes before, and have even hinted at possible solutions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252659,
    "dDurationMs": 2381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- we’ve never actually solved them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255040,
    "dDurationMs": 2729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s what we’ll try to do today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257769,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, the black hole paradoxes stem\nfrom the disagreement between quantum mechanics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261209,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263069,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The impossibility of the central singularity\nis the most obvious place where we need a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267379,
    "dDurationMs": 2081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory of quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269460,
    "dDurationMs": 3199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is also the problem at the event\nhorizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272659,
    "dDurationMs": 4536,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A theory of quantum gravity would have really helped Stephen Hawking in "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277195,
    "dDurationMs": 2615,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deriving his eponymous radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279810,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But such a theory didn’t exist back then\nin the 70s and still doesn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284180,
    "dDurationMs": 5239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead Hawking used a hack - something called the semi-classical approximation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289419,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He was able to describe the black hole using pure general relativity but then analyze its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295180,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect on the surrounding quantum fields,\nwhich only worked if the gravity at the horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300610,
    "dDurationMs": 5709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was relatively weak, in which case quantum\ngravity effects shouldn’t play a part."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306319,
    "dDurationMs": 3401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that assumption may be wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309720,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If gravity starts to get quantum even above\nthe event horizon, then it may be possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314780,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to actually encode information on that surface in a way that saves that information from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320030,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "destruction as the black hole evaporates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it turns out that our most advanced theory of quantum gravity can do this quite neatly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328720,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black hole paradoxes may be solved by string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332220,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In string theory, black holes are not hairless\nat all - in fact all of those strings make"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337169,
    "dDurationMs": 2261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them positively fuzzy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339430,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so we come to fuzzballs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341800,
    "dDurationMs": 2119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, very nearly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343919,
    "dDurationMs": 1431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First a spot of string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345350,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve covered this horrendously complex\ntopic in detail before, so I’m only going"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350330,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to give you the crudest overview."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352530,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In string theory, all elementary particles\nare oscillations in 1-dimensional strands"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357449,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that themselves are embedded within complex,\nmany-dimensional spaces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361819,
    "dDurationMs": 3831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "String theory immediately solves the problem of the black hole singularity, because instead"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365650,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of collapsing all of a black hole’s mass\ninto a single point, it gets distributed around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369919,
    "dDurationMs": 2401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ring structure of these strings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372320,
    "dDurationMs": 5099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lose that pesky infinite density and we can\nstart making sense of physics again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377419,
    "dDurationMs": 3761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that though, that string theory\ncan make sense of the black hole event horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381180,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382180,
    "dDurationMs": 5739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first step towards this was in 1996, when Andrew Strominger and Cumrun Vafa created"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387919,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a black hole using string theory - in theory-space, not actually."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393919,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I’ll come back to the some of details\nlater - for now just the result."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397849,
    "dDurationMs": 5141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These guys found a way to count the microstates on the horizon- the number of possible configurations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402990,
    "dDurationMs": 6940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stringy structures - the 1-D strings themselves and higher-dimensional structures called D-branes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409930,
    "dDurationMs": 5209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the number they found exactly agreed with the Bekenstein formula for black hole entropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415139,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was hard to believe that this was a mere\ncoincidence — it seemed they’d stumbled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419689,
    "dDurationMs": 4931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the mechanism by which information could be encoded on the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424620,
    "dDurationMs": 5849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The infinitesimal strings and branes of string theory might be the analog of the molecules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430469,
    "dDurationMs": 2931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that store the entropy of our room full of\nair."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should add that Strominger and Vafa did\nthis for the somewhat unrealistic case of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438200,
    "dDurationMs": 6189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a black hole horizon with 4 spatial dimensions, but it was a decisive step and it gave us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444389,
    "dDurationMs": 3857,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a compelling reason to think that string theory might explain where the "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448246,
    "dDurationMs": 2889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microstates of black holes live."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451135,
    "dDurationMs": 4645,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in order to solve the information paradox, we still have to get that information out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455780,
    "dDurationMs": 2729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the black hole as it evaporates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458509,
    "dDurationMs": 1611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The breakthrough came quickly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460120,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A year after the Strominger-Vafa result, Samir Mathur at Ohio State dug into that model to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466590,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see if it could reproduce properties of black\nholes beyond the entropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470560,
    "dDurationMs": 3009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it did, almost too well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473569,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For instance, the profile of the radiation\nemitted by the strings precisely matched that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478840,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of traditional Hawking radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480930,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was another stunning match to theory,\nand also a way for stringy black holes to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485199,
    "dDurationMs": 1601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leak out their information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486800,
    "dDurationMs": 5639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was looking pretty good, but I realize\nthat I haven’t really told you what these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492439,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stringy black holes look like, or how they\nform, or how structure can actually be supported"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497639,
    "dDurationMs": 1131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498770,
    "dDurationMs": 3609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For that we need to finally get to the idea\nof the fuzzball."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502379,
    "dDurationMs": 4241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, a thing that’s weird about even regular\nblack holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506620,
    "dDurationMs": 5419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you were to dial up the strength of gravity,\nblack holes would get bigger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512039,
    "dDurationMs": 4321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s different to pretty much everything\nelse in the universe, which are crunched down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516360,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to smaller size if you increase the power\nof gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519290,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This property of black holes is actually quite hard to reproduce in theories of quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525010,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But while he was exploring stringy black holes, Samir Mathur found that the strings that formed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530590,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole would increase in size as the\nstrength of gravity increased."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535190,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, if you have a bunch of strings dense\nenough to form a black hole in general relativity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540440,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it wouldn’t actually collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542110,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather it would grow to produce an agglomerate of strings with the same radius as a classical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548010,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549010,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If this is right, then black holes don’t\nhave an empty event horizon at all, but rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554301,
    "dDurationMs": 6459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a real surface that looks like a tangled nightmare of strings and branes, like the hairball coughed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560760,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up from some hyperdimensional quantum cat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564070,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A fuzzball."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565070,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just as the neutron star’s gravitational\nfield is so intense that atomic nuclei are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569930,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crushed into a soup of neutrons, a star collapsing into a fuzzball will see its constituents"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576200,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crushed into a soup of elementary strings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579700,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most amazing element of the fuzzball paradigm is the discovery that quantum gravity effects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585890,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might not just be important at the center\nof the black hole but instead may pile up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590570,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the horizon scale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592430,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ability for strings to maintain structure\nin this extreme gravity comes down to an effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597770,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specific to string theory called fractionation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602090,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The tension of a string is inversely proportional to its length: tight rubber bands are naturally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607840,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small because they are difficult to stretch\nand vice versa."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610880,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in string theory, when you merge a large\nnumber of strings, the tension of the resulting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616250,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object is effectively smaller than for\neach individual string - proportional to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620890,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of strings merged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622810,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means by crushing an enormous number of strings together, the resulting monstrosity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627480,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be stretched to enormous scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630410,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The normally Planck-length strings can therefore pile up into fuzzballs with sizes ranging"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635650,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from kilometers to light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637930,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The resulting fuzzball doesn’t have a local\nevent horizon - in that there’s no single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643410,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface of no-return."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646160,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But from a distance, fuzzballs would look\nlike black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650040,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light trying to escape would still be massively redshifted - sapped of energy by the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654450,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field - rendering the object effectively black."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658350,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would still cause massive gravitational\nlensing, time dilation, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662730,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All classical effects of black holes from\ngeneral relativity would be preserved."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667240,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you approach the fuzzball you start\nto see this surface of stringy material with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671980,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a thickness of about a Planck length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674880,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And what happens if you punch through that\nsurface?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677870,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, that’s where things get properly weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681220,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact there is no interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684240,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A fuzzball is really a fuzzsphere, or fuzzshell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688430,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not that the interior is empty, but\nrather that space and time literally end at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694560,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695560,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a fuzzball is forming, all of the matter\n- now dissolved into stringy mess, is pushed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701230,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up to the surface and the interior grid of\nspacetime is deleted from the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706770,
    "dDurationMs": 4481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This last thing is the weirdest and coolest\nthing about fuzzballs, so let’s explore"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711251,
    "dDurationMs": 3259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bit further by dropping a few dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714510,
    "dDurationMs": 7181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead of the 4 spatial dimensions of a Strominger-Vafa or the 3 dimensions of regular black holes, let’s think"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721691,
    "dDurationMs": 2559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about a 1-D black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724250,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One dimension means a line, so a 1-D black\nhole is just a segment of a line with a point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729210,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of infinite density on it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730980,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The event horizon is just the pair of equidistant points on the line inside of which escape"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735910,
    "dDurationMs": 1170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737080,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now let’s try a string-theoretic version."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740900,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In string theory we have extra compact dimensions - spatial dimensions that are coiled up on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746400,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Planck scale so we can’t see them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749130,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Adding a single coiled dimension to our 1-D\nblack hole turns our line into a drinking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754330,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "straw."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755330,
    "dDurationMs": 3270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The extra dimension is the distance around\nthe straw."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758600,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a fuzzball, spacetime closes on itself\nat the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763500,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The extra compact dimensions contract and pinch off, so all spatial dimensions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768510,
    "dDurationMs": 1830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end in that direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770340,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a side benefit, this eliminates the problem of the gravitational singularity at the center"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774530,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775870,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is no central singularity because there is no center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780160,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The emergence of fuzzballs in string theory\nradically changed our vision of what a black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784360,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole could be and provided a satisfying potential resolution to several paradoxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790770,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, this picture is not yet complete."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793130,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The model has only been carried out for simplified and nonrealistic-cases like the "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797469,
    "dDurationMs": 2484,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Strominger-Vafa black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799953,
    "dDurationMs": 5297,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Constructing fuzzballs that more closely match real astrophysical black holes remains a major"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805250,
    "dDurationMs": 1060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theoretical effort."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806310,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should also add that fuzzballs are not the\nonly quantum extension of the GR black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812090,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can come back to those another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815290,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes, or something like them, definitely exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818740,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And they’re one of our best hopes for understanding the union of general relativity with the quantum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823680,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps that’ll come when we find evidence\nof fuzziness surrounding those distant holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829640,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the fabric of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831310,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833440,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand astrophysics, Brilliant believes it helps to have a solid understanding of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838290,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839290,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant has a lesson on special relativity\nthat includes interactive challenges and problems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843550,
    "dDurationMs": 1030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to solve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844580,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hands-on approach can guide you through thinking strategies for challenging subjects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848980,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850840,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It starts out explaining relativity in terms\nof boats passing each other on a river,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854750,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then goes into some puzzles that require changing perspective (like the ball stack bounce),"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860550,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then goes into the speed of light and\neven time travel!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864430,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more about Brilliant, go to Brilliant.org/Spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869321,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone, I’d like to invite you to\nparticipate in PBS Digital Studios’ annual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874100,
    "dDurationMs": 1420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "audience survey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875520,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Space Time audience has always had a great showing on the annual survey and we’d love"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880650,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for that to continue this year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your feedback really helps us understand what\nyou’re interested in and you even get to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887200,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vote on potential new shows!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889290,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we really do spend quite a bit of time\nanalyzing and discussing the results."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894130,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a link in the description below."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896230,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you have a few minutes, we’d love your\ninput."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898290,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, as always, if you've joined us on Patreon, we can't emphasize enough how helpful your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902690,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support is in keeping this show going."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905140,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today, on behalf of one of our generous\ncontributors, we want to give a very special"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910480,
    "dDurationMs": 7230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang shoutout dedicated to the memory of Ernest H Anderson Jr. Earnest was a geophysicist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917710,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who’s PhD examined Martian Atmospheric Haze and he worked with the most famous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922420,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planetary scientist of all - Carl Sagan."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925250,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earnest was also a part of the Apollo XI mission, working in the Air Force in Colorado tracking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930380,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and predicting solar radiation bursts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933440,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We tend to talk about the famous scientists\non this show, but it’s the dedication, passion,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938620,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and hard work of scientists like Earnest that\nreally carry the grand scientific project"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944570,
    "dDurationMs": 1426,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for all humanity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945996,
    "dDurationMs": 6732,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it is our honor to honor Ernest H Anderson Jr., who passed away in 2018, and has returned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952728,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954358,
    "dDurationMs": 5242,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’re going to take a short release break\nto take a breath and get some mind-bending"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959600,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episodes written."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961220,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We will return in December with said episodes, and with the comments responses from last"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967140,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode - because we’re also taking a comments break."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970590,
    "dDurationMs": 1924,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See you all very soon."
    } ]
  } ]
}
